// ────────────── Output Configuration ──────────────
// https://github.com/YaLTeR/niri/wiki/Configuration:-Outputs
//
// SEED FILE. The `create_` source prefix means chezmoi writes this once, on a
// machine that has no ~/.config/niri/cfg/display.kdl yet, and never touches it
// again. Edit the copy in $HOME freely -- `chezmoi apply` will not revert it,
// and your monitor layout is never committed back to this repo.
//
// It exists mainly so `include "./cfg/display.kdl"` in config.kdl always
// resolves: niri treats a missing include as a hard config error, so leaving
// display.kdl unmanaged would break a fresh checkout outright.
//
// Connector names, modes, scale and placement are physical facts about one
// machine, so there is deliberately no portable default here.
//
// ── How placement works ──
// Niri has no "left-of" / "right-of" relation. Every output is placed by
// absolute logical coordinates, where `position x= y=` is that output's
// top-left corner in a single shared coordinate space. Pick one screen as the
// origin (0,0) -- usually the internal panel -- and position the others
// relative to it. Negative values are legal, and are how you place a screen to
// the left of, or above, the origin.
//
// With no `position`, niri auto-arranges outputs left-to-right, which can
// shift when you replug a monitor or add a third one. Set positions
// explicitly if you want the arrangement to stay put.
//
// Run `niri msg outputs` to list connectors and their modes, then
// `niri validate` after editing.
//
// ── Example: a 1920-wide external to the LEFT of a 1920x1200 laptop ──
// x = -1920 puts the external's right edge exactly on the laptop's left edge.
//
//   +----------++----------+
//   | HDMI-A-1 ||  eDP-1   |
//   +----------++----------+
//   x=-1920     x=0
//
// output "eDP-1" {
//     mode "1920x1200@60.003"
//     scale 1
//     position x=0 y=0
// }
//
// output "HDMI-A-1" {
//     mode "1920x1080@60.000"
//     scale 1
//     position x=-1920 y=0
// }
//
// Stack that external ABOVE instead with position x=0 y=-1080, or put it to
// the RIGHT with x=1920 y=0. Omit `mode` on an external you swap often so niri
// falls back to the preferred mode instead of warning it is unavailable.
